= Ground Transportation and Rail

== Transit Philosophy and Environmental Priorities

Ground transportation choices impact both partnership expenditure and our collective environmental footprint. Noice & Toit LLP prioritizes safe, reliable, and sustainable transit solutions. We encourage all personnel to utilize public transport networks where well-developed and safe, reserving taxis and rideshares for situations where time constraints or logistics demand them.

== Rail Travel as the Preferred Regional Option

Modern rail transit often provides a faster, more productive journey than air travel when factoring in airport security queues and ground transfers.

#card-do("Book train travel for journeys under four hours")[
  Choose rail transit when traveling between well-connected metropolitan centers such as London to Paris, Frankfurt to Berlin, Zurich to Geneva, or New York to Washington DC. Trains offer uninterrupted productive work environments, power outlets, and downtown-to-downtown convenience.
]

#card-do("Use standard second class as the default")[
  Second Class (Standard Class) is the firm standard for rail travel and is reimbursed without further justification. First Class is permitted only when the scheduled journey between the origin and destination stations printed on the ticket exceeds four (4) hours. A First Class ticket on a shorter route is rejected in full, because the ticket shows no Second Class fare that Finance could reimburse instead.
]

=== Seat Reservations and Flexible Fares
Seat reservation fees and flexible (refundable or rebookable) fares are reimbursable in full. Travelers should choose saver fares when the schedule is firm, but Finance does not deduct the difference.

=== Rail Season Passes and Transit Cards
If your project requires daily or weekly regional rail travel, request a temporary regional travel card (such as a German BahnCard, Swiss Half-Fare card, or UK Season ticket) through the Travel Desk. Finance will reimburse the pass cost if the projected ticket savings exceed the card purchase price.

== City Transit, Taxis, and Ride-Hailing

Public transit systems (subways, underground networks, trams, and airport express trains) are the preferred mode of city travel across London, Berlin, New York, Zurich, and Singapore.

#card-do("Use public transit when practical and safe")[
  Use airport express rail links (such as the Heathrow Express, Berlin FEX, or JFK AirTrain) and city subway lines for routine travel between hotels, offices, and client locations. Upload ticket stubs or contactless payment receipts to your expense report.
]

#card-do("Take standard taxis or ride-shares when justified")[
  A taxi or standard ride-hailing fare (UberX, Lyft Standard, FreeNow, licensed street taxis) is reimbursable in full when either the fare before tip is *€25.00 / \$30.00 / £22.00 or less*, or the pickup or drop-off is an airport or long-distance rail station, or the submission note states one of the following justifications:
  - Transporting bulky presentation displays, trial bundles, or heavy client equipment.
  - Travel after 21:00 or before 06:00 local time (the receipt time governs).
  - Consecutive offsite meetings where public transit would cause schedule failure.
  - Several employees sharing one vehicle, making it cheaper than individual transit tickets.
  A fare above the threshold that satisfies none of these is reimbursed up to the threshold and the excess is deducted.
]

#card-dont("Book premium or luxury rideshare categories")[
  Never order premium rideshare tiers such as Uber Black, Uber Lux, Lyft Lux, or private chauffeur services. Where the receipt identifies a premium tier, the fare is reimbursed up to the unjustified-ride threshold above and the excess is deducted.
]

=== Tips on Taxi Fares
Taxi tips follow the Global Tipping Standards in Chapter 14, calculated on the fare before tip: up to 20% in Germany and in the United States and Canada, up to 10% elsewhere in the European Union and in Switzerland, nothing in Japan, South Korea, Singapore, or Hong Kong. Tip amounts above the local cap are deducted.

== Rental Cars

Rental cars should be considered only when public transit, rail, and rideshare options are unavailable, or when visiting multiple rural client sites in a single day. Employees must book rental cars through our corporate portal with preferred partners (Hertz, Enterprise, National). The authorized booking class is *Compact* or *Intermediate*; electric or hybrid vehicles in those classes are encouraged. Full-size vehicles, SUVs, and luxury sedans require the approving Practice Leader to be named in the note.

#card-do("Refuel the vehicle before returning it")[
  Always refuel the vehicle at a standard petrol station before returning it to the rental depot. Rental car agency refueling charges include substantial markup fees. Keep the itemized fuel receipt to claim reimbursement.
]

#card-dont("Accept optional prepaid fuel or navigation packages")[
  Do not purchase optional prepaid fuel packages, satellite navigation add-ons, or satellite radio subscriptions. Modern smartphones provide comprehensive navigation without extra rental fees.
]

== Fuel Receipts

Fuel is reimbursed only for rental or firm pool vehicles. Fuel for a personal vehicle is never reimbursed; personal vehicle usage is compensated through the mileage allowance below, which already covers fuel.

#card-do("Identify the rental vehicle in the note")[
  When submitting a petrol station receipt, state in the note that the fuel was for a rental or pool vehicle and name the trip or client site. The fuel line (liters multiplied by price per liter) is then reimbursed in full. Commercial EV charging receipts are treated the same way.
]

#card-dont("Claim non-fuel items from the petrol station shop")[
  Coffee, snacks, drinks, car washes, windscreen fluid, and any other shop items printed on a fuel receipt are deducted. If the note does not identify a rental or pool vehicle, Finance treats the vehicle as personal and rejects the fuel receipt in full.
]

== Personal Vehicle Usage and Mileage Reimbursement

Employees may use their personal vehicles for approved business travel when other transit forms are impractical. Mileage is claimed through the portal's mileage log, not with fuel receipts.

#card-do("Log business mileage accurately")[
  Reimbursement for personal vehicle usage is calculated at fixed statutory rates:
  - *European Union*: €0.38 per kilometer.
  - *United States*: \$0.67 per mile (IRS conforming rate).
  - *United Kingdom*: £0.45 per mile up to 10,000 miles per tax year.
  This rate covers all fuel, oil, regular maintenance, tire wear, insurance coverage, and vehicle depreciation. To claim mileage, provide a log listing the date, departure address, destination address, client matter, and verified map distance.
]

#card-dont("Claim personal commuting miles")[
  Never claim mileage or transit fares for daily travel between your home residence and your regular assigned office. Commuting is a personal living expense under statutory tax law and cannot be reimbursed or deducted by the firm.
]

=== Collision Damage and Rental Insurance
- *Within North America*: Decline optional Collision Damage Waiver (CDW) and Loss Damage Waiver (LDW) when renting with the corporate credit card, as comprehensive coverage is provided centrally through our firm insurance program. Optional waivers that appear on a rental invoice anyway are reimbursed; the Travel Desk will follow up with a gentle reminder.
- *International Rentals*: In European and Asian jurisdictions where statutory third-party liability and comprehensive insurance are not covered by corporate cards, select the standard mandatory CDW coverage at the rental counter.

== A Word on Bicycles, Scooters, and Other Wheels

Shared bicycles and e-scooters are welcome for short hops between offices, stations, and client sites, and their app receipts are reimbursable like public transit. Helmets are strongly encouraged and not reimbursable. The Berlin office notes that the cobblestones of Prenzlauer Berg have ended more scooter journeys than any policy ever could.

== Parking Fees, Tolls, and Congestion Charges

- *Reimbursable*: Business-related toll charges, municipal congestion charges (e.g., London Congestion Charge), public garage or airport long-term parking during multi-day assignments, and hotel parking for a rental or pool vehicle up to the nightly cap in Chapter 6.
- *Non-Reimbursable*: Traffic or parking citation penalties (Chapter 11), and parking for a personal vehicle at your regular office.

